    content: `In cricket, a NO-BALL and a WIDE are both illegal deliveries that (1) add one run (an "extra") to the batting team's score, and (2) do not count toward the six legal balls of the over, so the bowler must bowl an extra delivery. They differ in WHY the delivery is illegal. A NO-BALL is a delivery bowled in an unfair or unsafe manner. The most frequent cause is a FRONT-FOOT FAULT: the bowler's front foot must land with some part of it behind the popping crease — if the whole foot is over the line ("overstepping"), it's a no-ball (now checked by the third umpire in top-level cricket). Other no-balls: a full-toss (non-bouncing delivery) that passes above the batter's waist height (a "beamer"), a delivery that bounces more than twice (or rolls) before reaching the batter, throwing/chucking (illegal straightening of the arm), having more fielders behind square on the leg side than allowed, the wicketkeeper encroaching, or a back-foot fault. In limited-overs cricket a no-ball also earns the batting side a FREE HIT on the following delivery, on which the striker can only be dismissed run-out (or by the ways that also apply to a no-ball). Off a no-ball, the batter cannot be bowled, caught, LBW, stumped, or hit-wicket, but CAN be run out; any runs scored off the bat also count. A WIDE is called when the bowler delivers the ball so wide of the batter and the stumps — down the leg side or well outside off — that, in the umpire's judgement, the batter cannot reach it to play a normal cricketing stroke from where they are standing. The standard is applied much more strictly in limited-overs cricket (there are marked "wide guides" on the pitch), and especially harshly for balls down the leg side. Off a wide, the batter CANNOT be bowled, caught, or LBW, but CAN be stumped, run out, hit-wicket, or out for handling/obstruction; if byes are run or the ball goes to the boundary, those runs are added to the one wide run.`,
