    content: `In basketball, a PERSONAL FOUL and a TECHNICAL FOUL are different categories of infraction. A PERSONAL FOUL is illegal physical CONTACT with an opponent while the ball is live: holding, pushing, charging, blocking, hitting the arm on a shot, an illegal (moving) screen, "reaching in," tripping, or an illegal hand-check. It is charged against the individual player who committed it and counts toward the number that causes a player to "foul out" of the game — SIX in the NBA, FIVE in college (NCAA), FIBA, and most high-school rules. Consequences depend on the situation: a common foul away from the ball before the team is "in the bonus" just gives the other team the ball out of bounds; a foul on a player in the act of shooting gives free throws (two, or three if beyond the arc; one if the shot went in); and once a team has committed enough team fouls in a period, the opponent is "in the bonus" and shoots free throws on every subsequent non-shooting foul. A TECHNICAL FOUL ("a T") is assessed for MISCONDUCT or for breaking an administrative rule, and usually involves NO physical contact with an opponent: arguing with, showing up, or making contact with a referee; taunting, baiting, or excessive trash-talk; unsportsmanlike conduct; hanging on the rim (except to avoid injury); delay of game; a defensive three-second violation (NBA); having too many players on the floor; or a coach/bench personnel misbehaving. Penalty: the opposing team gets FREE THROW(S) — one in the NBA (since 2020–21) or two in NCAA — AND, in the NBA, retains possession of the ball. A player who receives TWO technical fouls in one game is automatically EJECTED (and fines/suspensions can follow from accumulation over a season). Related but separate: a FLAGRANT foul is contact that is unnecessary and/or excessive/violent — it gives free throws plus possession, and a flagrant-2 means ejection.`,
