    content: `In soccer (association football), YELLOW and RED cards are the referee's tools for punishing misconduct, defined in Law 12 of the Laws of the Game. A YELLOW CARD is a CAUTION — an official, recorded warning. The player REMAINS on the pitch and keeps playing. It is shown for offences such as: unsporting behaviour (including a reckless foul, simulation/diving, or a tactical "professional" foul that isn't a goal-scoring chance), dissent by word or action toward an official, persistent infringement of the Laws, delaying the restart of play (time-wasting), failing to respect the required distance at a free kick or corner, entering/re-entering or leaving the field without the referee's permission, and a deliberate handball that stops a promising attack. A RED CARD is a SENDING-OFF (ejection). The player must leave the field of play (and the technical area) immediately and CANNOT be substituted — the team must play the remainder of the match with one fewer player (10 against 11). A red is shown for: serious foul play (a challenge endangering an opponent's safety), violent conduct (striking, elbowing, headbutting), spitting at anyone, denying the opposing team a goal by handling the ball, denying an obvious goal-scoring opportunity by a foul ("last-man" foul or DOGSO), and using offensive, insulting, or threatening language or gestures. KEY RULE: TWO YELLOW cards in the same match automatically produce a RED card, and that player is sent off (shown as second yellow, then red). A straight red also carries a SUSPENSION for one or more subsequent matches, the length depending on the offence. Separately, yellow cards ACCUMULATE across a competition — reaching a set total (e.g. two yellows in a group stage, or five over a league season) triggers a one-match ban; in knockout tournaments, accumulated yellows are typically wiped clean after the quarter-finals so a final booking doesn't rule a player out of the final.`,
