    content: `In badminton, the difference between SINGLES and DOUBLES is the NUMBER OF PLAYERS on each side — NOT the number of rackets (every player always holds exactly ONE racket). SINGLES = one player versus one player. DOUBLES = two players versus two players (MIXED DOUBLES is one man and one woman per side). The rules that change between the two formats: (1) COURT DIMENSIONS. The court is 13.4 m long and 6.1 m wide overall, with "tramlines" (a narrow lane) down each side and across the back. In SINGLES, the side tramlines are OUT of bounds, so the court is narrower (5.18 m wide), but the full length to the very back line is IN — a long, narrow court. In DOUBLES, the full width INCLUDING the side tramlines is IN (6.1 m), making the court wider, but for the SERVE ONLY, the back tramline is out, so serves must land in a slightly shorter box. (2) SERVICE. In both formats you serve from the right service court when your score is even and the left when it is odd. In singles this is straightforward. In doubles, only the serving pair swaps sides (and only when they win a point while serving); the receiving pair does not rotate. Doubles serves must be low and land within the wider-but-shorter doubles service court, and are almost always a "low serve" or "flick." (3) STYLE OF PLAY. Doubles rallies are far faster and more attacking, with partners rotating between a front-and-back (attacking) formation and a side-by-side (defending) formation. Singles is more about court coverage, stamina, and moving the opponent around the corners. Scoring is the same: rally scoring to 21 points, win by 2, cap at 30, best of three games.`,
