    content: `FORMULA 1 (F1) and INDYCAR are both the pinnacle of OPEN-WHEEL, single-seat motorsport, but they are run on very different philosophies. FORMULA 1 is a GLOBAL championship and, crucially, a CONSTRUCTORS' formula: each of the ~10 teams must DESIGN AND BUILD ITS OWN car (chassis, aerodynamics, much of the drivetrain) to a detailed technical rulebook. This makes F1 a technology and spending race — the best-funded, best-engineered teams (recently Red Bull, Mercedes, Ferrari, McLaren) dominate. The cars use ~1.6-litre turbocharged V6 HYBRID power units (from Ferrari, Mercedes, Honda/RBPT, and soon Audi) producing roughly 1,000 hp, and are the fastest road-course racing cars in the world (huge downforce, carbon brakes). F1 races almost entirely on permanent ROAD COURSES and temporary STREET CIRCUITS across ~24 countries; it has no ovals. Refuelling during races has been banned since 2010. IndyCar (the NTT IndyCar Series) is a largely AMERICAN series and a "SPEC" (control) formula: every team runs the SAME chassis (built by Dallara) with a common aero kit, and one of only two engine suppliers — CHEVROLET or HONDA — both ~2.2-litre twin-turbo V6s making ~550–700 hp (more with "push-to-pass" boost). Because the equipment is nearly identical and far cheaper, the racing is closer and a smaller team can win. IndyCar's signature feature is that it races on a MIX of circuit types in one season: giant SUPERSPEEDWAY OVALS (Indianapolis, where cars top 240 mph), short ovals, natural road courses, and street circuits — demanding very different setups and skills. Its crown-jewel event is the INDIANAPOLIS 500 (part of the "Triple Crown" of motorsport). IndyCar uses rolling starts on ovals and standing or rolling starts on road courses, allows in-race refuelling, and generally produces far more on-track overtaking than F1, which relies on DRS (a drag-reduction rear wing) and pit strategy.`,
