    content: `A BALK and a WILD PITCH both let base runners advance, but they are completely different things. A BALK is an ILLEGAL PITCHING MOTION, called only when there is at least one runner on base. It is NOT about the pitcher's intent — it is about violating the specific rules that govern how a pitcher may move, which exist to stop the pitcher from unfairly DECEIVING the runners. Common balks: beginning the windup or the delivery motion and then stopping or altering it; failing to come to a complete, discernible STOP in the "set position" before delivering; faking a pickoff throw to first or third base (the pitcher may fake to second); throwing or feinting to a base without stepping directly toward it; stepping toward home to pitch but throwing to a base (or vice versa); dropping the ball while on the rubber; "quick-pitching" before the batter is set; or the pitcher standing on or astride the rubber without the ball. PENALTY: the ball is dead and EACH runner is awarded ONE base automatically; if a pitch was thrown, it doesn't count (unless all runners including the batter advance at least one base on the play, in which case the play stands). A WILD PITCH is a LEGALLY delivered pitch that is so errant — sailing high, wide, or bouncing in the dirt past the catcher — that the catcher cannot stop it with ordinary effort, allowing runner(s) already on base to advance. It is charged to the PITCHER as an official statistic. The closely related PASSED BALL is a pitch the catcher SHOULD have handled but failed to (a drop or misplay), allowing runners to advance — that one is charged to the CATCHER as an error-like stat. On a wild pitch or passed ball the ball is LIVE: runners advance at their own risk and can be tagged or thrown out. SUMMARY: a balk is a rules infraction with an automatic one-base award (no pitch needed); a wild pitch is a genuine but uncontrollable pitch where runners choose to advance and could be put out.`,
