    content: `The DECATHLON and the HEPTATHLON are "combined events" (multi-events) in athletics: an athlete competes in a fixed sequence of track and field events over two consecutive days, earns points for each performance from standardised IAAF/World Athletics scoring tables, and the athlete with the highest POINTS TOTAL wins. The core difference is the number of events and, traditionally, the gender contesting it. The DECATHLON is TEN events, contested outdoors, historically and at the Olympics by MEN (women's decathlon exists but is rare). The order is fixed: DAY 1 — 100 metres, long jump, shot put, high jump, 400 metres. DAY 2 — 110 m hurdles, discus throw, pole vault, javelin throw, 1500 metres. The reigning Olympic decathlon champion is traditionally dubbed "the world's greatest athlete." A total above 9000 points is elite (the world record is over 9300). The HEPTATHLON is SEVEN events, contested outdoors primarily by WOMEN. The order: DAY 1 — 100 m hurdles, high jump, shot put, 200 metres. DAY 2 — long jump, javelin throw, 800 metres. An elite women's heptathlon score is roughly 6500–7000+ points (world record ~7291). NOTE: there is also a MEN'S INDOOR heptathlon — a different set of seven events (60 m, long jump, shot put, high jump / 60 m hurdles, pole vault, 1000 m) contested at world indoor championships — and a WOMEN'S INDOOR PENTATHLON (five events). Women formerly did an outdoor PENTATHLON (five events); it was expanded to the seven-event heptathlon for international competition in 1981.`,
