    content: `GRECO-ROMAN and FREESTYLE are the two styles of amateur/Olympic wrestling, both governed by United World Wrestling (UWW). They share the same objective — win by PIN (fall — pressing both of the opponent's shoulder blades to the mat) or, failing that, on POINTS scored for takedowns, turning ("exposure" of the opponent's back), reversals, and holds — contested on a circular mat over two 3-minute periods (or as scored). The one crucial rule difference: GRECO-ROMAN prohibits ALL HOLDS BELOW THE WAIST, and prohibits using the LEGS to execute or defend any hold (no leg trips, no hooking, no grapevines). Every attack must be UPPER-BODY only — collar ties, underhooks, body locks, arm drags — which is why Greco-Roman is famous for big, explosive THROWS, SUPLEXES, and high-amplitude lifts, and for grinding upper-body position battles. It is an Olympic event for MEN only. FREESTYLE ALLOWS attacking the legs and using your own legs: single-leg and double-leg takedowns, ankle picks, high crotches, leg laces, and grapevines are all legal. This makes freestyle much closer to American "folkstyle" (scholastic and NCAA collegiate) wrestling — though folkstyle emphasises riding time and control on the mat, while freestyle rewards exposure and back points. Freestyle is an Olympic event for BOTH men and women. THE QUICK ANSWER: in Greco-Roman you cannot touch the legs; in freestyle you can.`,
