    content: `In rugby union, a SCRUM and a RUCK are both contests for the ball involving bound players, but one is a formal restart and the other is open play. A SCRUM is a SET PIECE — a structured restart of play ordered by the REFEREE after a minor, non-deliberate infringement: most commonly a KNOCK-ON (a player drops or knocks the ball forward with hand or arm) or a FORWARD PASS, but also when the ball becomes unplayable in a ruck or maul, or a player is accidentally offside. The eight FORWARDS of each team bind together in a set formation of three rows (front row of three, second row of two, back row of three), the two packs engage and lock together on the referee's calls ("crouch, bind, set"), and the SCRUM-HALF of the team awarded the scrum rolls the ball into the "tunnel" between the front rows. Both packs then push and the hookers try to "hook" the ball backward with a foot to their own side, from where the scrum-half retrieves it. Backs must stay 5 m behind the hindmost foot until the ball is out. A RUCK is a phase of LOOSE, CONTINUOUS play that forms SPONTANEOUSLY (no referee whistle) after a TACKLE: the ball-carrier is brought to ground, the tackler must release, and then one or more players from EACH team, on their feet and in contact, close over the ball. Once a ruck has formed, players may NOT handle the ball with their hands — they must try to drive the opposition backward off the ball ("clearing out"/"rucking") with their bodies and feet, and the ball is then played from the back of the ruck by the scrum-half. An offside line runs through the hindmost foot of each side's ruck. RELATED: a MAUL is like a ruck but the ball-carrier is still ON THEIR FEET, held by opponents, with team-mates bound on — it must keep moving or the ball must come out or a scrum is awarded. SUMMARY: scrum = referee-ordered 8-v-8 restart; ruck = unplanned, hands-off contest on the ground over a tackled player.`,
