    content: `BUTTERFLY and BREASTSTROKE are both SYMMETRICAL swimming strokes — both arms move together (mirrored), and both legs move together, unlike the alternating action of front crawl and backstroke — and in both the swimmer is chest-down. But the mechanics, and the speed, are very different. BUTTERFLY ("fly"): the arms RECOVER (return to the front) TOGETHER and OVER the surface of the water, thrown forward in a wide circular sweep, then enter shoulder-width and pull down-and-back underwater in a keyhole/hourglass path, finishing past the hips. The legs perform a DOLPHIN KICK — both legs held together, whipping downward and upward driven from the hips and core, with the whole body UNDULATING like a wave. The timing is two dolphin kicks per arm cycle (one as the hands enter, one as they exit). Breathing is forward, lifting the chin as the arms sweep back. Butterfly is the SECOND-FASTEST competitive stroke (after front crawl) and the most physically exhausting. BREASTSTROKE: the arms stay IN the water (or barely break the surface) — they sweep OUT from in front of the chest, then IN and slightly down in a small heart-shaped scull, then SHOOT forward together to full extension. The legs do a WHIP KICK (or "frog kick"): the heels are drawn up toward the buttocks with the knees apart, the feet are turned out ("dorsiflexed"), and then swept outward, backward, and together in a circular whip, finishing with a squeeze and a GLIDE. Each full stroke has a distinct streamlined GLIDE phase. The head lifts to breathe every stroke as the arms sweep in. Breaststroke is the SLOWEST of the four competitive strokes, but the most stable and the easiest to swim slowly with the head up — which is why it's the usual "recreational" and lifesaving stroke. In competition, both strokes require a simultaneous two-hand touch at the turn and finish.`,
