    content: `A HAT-TRICK means the SAME THING at its core in both soccer and ice hockey: ONE PLAYER SCORING THREE GOALS in a single game. The term originates from CRICKET — in the 1850s–60s, a bowler who dismissed three batsmen with three consecutive deliveries was rewarded by his club with a new hat (or a collection was taken up to buy him one). It spread to other sports as a general term for a threefold individual achievement. IN SOCCER (association football): the three goals do NOT have to be consecutive or in the same half, and can be scored any way (open play, penalties, free kicks, even a deflection). A "PERFECT hat-trick" (or "natural" in some usages, though that term is more hockey) is three goals scored with the left foot, the right foot, and the head. There is no crowd ritual, but by long tradition the player is allowed to keep the MATCH BALL, often getting team-mates to sign it. IN ICE HOCKEY: again the three goals need not be consecutive. The distinctive tradition is that when the player scores their THIRD goal, FANS THROW HATS ONTO THE ICE in celebration, forcing a brief stoppage while the ice crew clears them (throwing other objects is penalised, but hats are tolerated for a hat-trick). A "NATURAL hat-trick" in hockey specifically means three consecutive goals by the same player in one game with NO goals by anyone else (either team) in between. There is also the informal "Gordie Howe hat-trick" — a goal, an assist, and a fight in the same game. So: same basic definition (3 goals, 1 player, 1 game); soccer keeps the ball, hockey gets hats on the ice.`,
