    content: `The TOUR DE FRANCE and the GIRO D'ITALIA are two of the three "GRAND TOURS" of professional road cycling — three-week-long stage races (roughly 21 stages over 23 days, ~3,300–3,500 km, with two rest days) in which the winner is the rider with the lowest cumulative time (the "general classification" or GC). The third Grand Tour is the Vuelta a España. THE TOUR DE FRANCE: held in JULY, almost entirely within France (with occasional starts — the "Grand Départ" — in neighbouring countries). First run in 1903, organised by ASO. It is the LARGEST and most PRESTIGIOUS bike race in the world by far — the biggest crowds, TV audience, budget, and history; winning it (or even a stage) is the pinnacle of the sport. The overall leader wears the YELLOW JERSEY ("maillot jaune") — yellow because the founding organiser's newspaper, L'Auto, was printed on yellow paper. Other classification jerseys: green (points/sprint), polka-dot (king of the mountains), white (best young rider). THE GIRO D'ITALIA: held in MAY, primarily in Italy. First run in 1909, organised by RCS Sport (publisher of the pink sports paper La Gazzetta dello Sport — hence the leader's PINK JERSEY, the "maglia rosa"). The Giro is regarded as the second-most prestigious Grand Tour and is famous for: more volatile, often brutal WEATHER (May snowstorms on the high Alpine and Dolomite passes have caused stages to be shortened or cancelled); a more EXPERIMENTAL and frequently HARDER route design, with steeper climbs (the "muri") and more mountain-top finishes; and, because many GC stars save themselves for the Tour, sometimes a slightly thinner field at the very top. Winning all three Grand Tours in a career is rare; winning the Giro and the Tour in the SAME year ("the Double") is one of cycling's greatest feats.`,
