    id: 'kb-gap-sport-caught-runout-cricket',
    title: "Caught vs Run Out (Cricket Dismissals)",
    category: 'Sports',
    keywords: [
      'what is the difference between caught and run out in cricket fielder catches a hit ball before it bounces batter out bowler credited versus fielders break the stumps while a batter is out of the ground going for a run no bowler credit',
      'CAUGHT is when the batter hits the ball with the bat or glove holding the bat and a fielder or the wicketkeeper catches it on the full before it touches the ground the striker is out the bowler gets the wicket in the stats and no runs are scored it is the most common form of dismissal',
      'RUN OUT is when the ball is live and a batter is OUT OF THEIR GROUND behind the popping crease usually while attempting a run or a mix up and a fielder breaks the WICKET puts the bails off with the ball the batter who has not made their ground is out',
      'key contrasts caught the ball never hit the ground and the dismissal is about the catch run out the batter is running and fails to reach the crease in time caught is credited to the BOWLER run out is NOT credited to any bowler it goes down as run out either batter can be run out including the non striker a run out can happen off a no ball a catch cannot dismiss off a no ball',
    ],
    content: `CAUGHT and RUN OUT are two ways a batter can be dismissed ("out") in cricket, and they are very different. CAUGHT: the batter hits the ball with the bat (or with a glove that is holding the bat) and a fielder — or the wicketkeeper — catches the ball ON THE FULL, before it touches the ground or any boundary. The STRIKER is out, no runs are scored from that ball (even if the batters had crossed), and the wicket is CREDITED TO THE BOWLER in the scorebook and statistics (e.g. "c Smith b Jones"). Caught is the most frequent mode of dismissal in cricket. Variants: caught behind (by the keeper), caught and bowled (by the bowler off their own delivery), caught at slip, in the deep, etc. A catch is not valid if the fielder steps on or over the boundary while holding the ball, or off a no-ball. RUN OUT: while the ball is IN PLAY (live), a batter is OUT OF THEIR GROUND — i.e. no part of their bat (grounded) or body is behind the popping crease at the end they are running to — and a fielder puts down the WICKET at that end (dislodges a bail with the ball, either by throwing and hitting the stumps or breaking them while holding the ball). The batter who has not made their ground is out. This usually happens on a misjudged quick single, a fumble, a direct-hit throw from the field, or a mix-up between the two batters. Crucially, a run out is NOT credited to any bowler — the scorebook just says "run out (fielder's name)". EITHER batter can be run out, including the NON-STRIKER (who can be run out backing up too far, the "Mankad"). A run out CAN occur off a no-ball or a wide (the ball is still live), whereas a batter cannot be caught, bowled, or LBW off a no-ball. So: caught = the hit ball is taken cleanly in the air, bowler's wicket; run out = a batter fails to complete a run and the stumps are broken, no bowler's wicket.`,
