    content: `The SHOT PUT and the DISCUS THROW are two of the four standard throwing events in track and field (the others are the hammer and the javelin). Both are contested from a circular concrete THROWING CIRCLE; the implement must land within a marked sector; the athlete may not touch the top of the circle rim or the ground outside it, and must not leave the circle until the implement has landed (and then must exit from the back half); each athlete gets six attempts and the best legal mark wins; distance is measured from the inside edge of the circle to the nearest mark made by the implement. THE SHOT PUT uses a solid, heavy METAL BALL ("the shot") — 7.26 kg (16 lb) for senior men, 4 kg for senior women. Critically, it must be PUT, not thrown: the shot is held against the side of the neck, under the jaw, and pushed away from the shoulder with one hand in a single motion (it may never drop behind the line of the shoulders). The circle is 2.135 m (7 ft) across with a curved STOP BOARD ("toe board") at the front. Athletes use one of two techniques: the GLIDE (a linear backward "O'Brien" shuffle across the circle) or the SPIN (a rotational, discus-like turn). Elite men put over 22 m; elite women over 20 m. THE DISCUS uses a flat, lens-shaped DISC — a wood or plastic body with a heavy metal rim — weighing 2 kg for men, 1 kg for women. It is SLUNG (thrown), released off the index finger with a flick, after the thrower makes ABOUT ONE-AND-A-HALF fast ROTATIONAL turns across a larger circle (2.5 m diameter) enclosed by a tall safety CAGE (the discus is dangerous if it flies off-line). There is no stop board. Elite men throw over 70 m and elite women over 65 m — more than three times the shot-put distance — because the spinning discus, released at the right angle into a headwind, generates aerodynamic LIFT and glides.`,
