    id: 'kb-gap-sport-corner-goal-kick',
    title: "Corner Kick vs Goal Kick (in Soccer)",
    category: 'Sports',
    keywords: [
      'what is the difference between a corner kick and a goal kick in soccer football ball crosses the goal line not into the goal last touched by defending team attacking corner arc versus last touched by attacking team defending team restart from the goal area',
      'BOTH restarts happen when the whole ball crosses the GOAL LINE the end line not the side touchline and not into the goal the difference is which team touched it last',
      'a CORNER KICK is awarded to the ATTACKING team when the ball crosses the goal line having last been touched by a DEFENDING player it is placed in the quarter circle arc at the nearest corner flag and kicked back into play a goal can be scored directly from a corner an Olympic goal',
      'a GOAL KICK is awarded to the DEFENDING team when the ball crosses the goal line having last been touched by an ATTACKING player and no goal was scored it is placed anywhere in the goal area the six yard box and since 2019 the ball is in play as soon as it is kicked it no longer has to leave the penalty area first',
    ],
    content: `A CORNER KICK and a GOAL KICK are both restarts used when the whole of the ball has passed over the GOAL LINE (the end line, between the corner flags — NOT the side "touchline," which produces a throw-in) WITHOUT a goal being scored. The difference is which team last touched the ball. A CORNER KICK is awarded to the ATTACKING team when the ball crossed the goal line and the LAST player to touch it was on the DEFENDING team (a defender's deflection, a save pushed wide/over by the goalkeeper, etc.). It is taken from within the QUARTER-CIRCLE ARC at whichever corner flag is on the side the ball went out; the ball must be stationary and the corner flag must not be moved; defenders must stay at least 9.15 m (10 yd) away until the kick is taken. A goal CAN be scored directly from a corner kick (a "gol olímpico" / Olympic goal — rare, but legal). A GOAL KICK is awarded to the DEFENDING team when the ball crossed the goal line and the LAST touch was by an ATTACKING player, and no goal resulted (e.g. a shot or cross that sails wide or over, or a through-ball that runs out). It is taken from anywhere within the GOAL AREA (the small "six-yard box"), usually by the goalkeeper but legally by any player. Since a 2019 Law change, the ball is IN PLAY as soon as it is kicked and clearly moves — it NO LONGER has to leave the penalty area before a team-mate can touch it — which is why teams now often play short goal kicks to a defender inside the box. Opponents must be outside the penalty area until the ball is in play. So: ball off a defender → corner to the attackers; ball off an attacker → goal kick to the defenders.`,
