    content: `The WNBA and the NBA play by MOSTLY THE SAME rules — five-on-five, a 24-second shot clock, the same 10-foot basket height, the same court dimensions (94 × 50 ft), the same three-point line distance (22 ft in the corners, 23 ft 9 in at the top — the WNBA moved to the NBA distance in 2013), the same lane, the same 8-second backcourt count, defensive three-seconds, and generally the same foul structure. The genuine differences are a handful of specifics: (1) GAME LENGTH — the WNBA plays 40 minutes, as FOUR 10-MINUTE quarters; the NBA plays 48 minutes, as four 12-minute quarters. (Both use 5-minute overtime periods.) (2) THE BALL — the WNBA uses a SIZE 6 ball, 28.5 inches in circumference and slightly lighter; the NBA (and men's basketball generally) uses a SIZE 7 ball, 29.5 inches. (3) ROSTERS — WNBA teams carry about 12 players (tight salary-cap constraints); NBA teams carry 15 plus two-way contract players. (4) SEASON — the WNBA regular season is roughly 40 games, played MAY to SEPTEMBER; the NBA plays 82 games, OCTOBER to APRIL. (5) Historically there were more gaps (e.g. the WNBA once used two 20-minute halves, and the leagues adopted the shot clock and various "point of emphasis" and "take foul" tweaks at slightly different times), but the modern products are very close. Most casual "difference" discussion about the WNBA and NBA is actually about REVENUE, salaries, and media coverage — not the on-court rules, which are largely aligned.`,
